<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wolfger von Erla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1140–<a href="/years/1218">1218</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wolfger von Erla was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a> from 1191 and patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> from 1204 until his death in 1218. A patron of poets, his travel accounts record a gift to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walther-von-der-vogelweide">Walther von der Vogelweide</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nibelungenlied">Nibelungenlied</a> is thought to have been composed at his court.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wolfger-von-erla.jpg" alt="Wolfger von Erla" width="320" height="158" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zecca di Aquileia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aquileia_-_Patriarca_Volchiero_-_image02504.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wolfger, known in Italian as Volchero, was born to a noble family from Erla on the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enns-river">Enns</a>. Early documents show him as a married layman with at least one son, Ottokar, and he probably entered minor orders as a widower. He became provost of Pfaffmünster near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straubing">Straubing</a> in 1183 and of Zell am See in 1184, then a canon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a> cathedral. Only after his election as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> was he ordained priest; he was consecrated by Archbishop Adalbert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>, and his son often travelled with him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a> he enlarged the diocese's holdings and was a steady ally of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Staufers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-babenberg">Babenbergs</a>. In 1195 he helped negotiate the release of Richard the Lionheart, held at Dürnstein, and Emperor Henry VI sent him to Pope Celestine III over the succession to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. He went on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a> of 1197 with Duke Frederick I of Austria, was among the German princes who made the hospital fraternity at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, and in 1199 obtained its papal charter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a>. In the throne dispute after 1197 he held to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-swabia">Philip of Swabia</a> and was excommunicated.</p><p>As patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> he helped open the negotiations between Philip, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Otto of Brunswick</a> and Innocent, and on 11 June 1206 Philip enfeoffed him with the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friuli">Friuli</a> and made him imperial legate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. After Philip's murder in 1208, Otto IV invested him with Istria on 8 May 1210, and he styled himself margrave of Istria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>, though Margrave Henry II kept real control of Carniola. He left Otto's service when the king claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Wolfger attended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-council-of-the-lateran">Fourth Lateran Council</a> in 1215 and then withdrew from imperial politics; his last act of diplomacy was the peace of April 1216 that ended the War of the Castle of Love between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, Treviso and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. He died on 23 January 1218, revered as a man of moderation and a patron of the arts. Besides his link with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walther-von-der-vogelweide">Walther von der Vogelweide</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nibelungenlied">Nibelungenlied</a>, the poet Albrecht von Johansdorf was associated with his court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a>, the jurist Eilbert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> dedicated his Ordo iudiciarius to him, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> he hosted Thomasin von Zirclaere and Boncompagno da Signa.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wolfger von Erla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Nibelungenlied may have been composed around 1200 at the court of Bishop <b>Wolfger von Erla</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-passau">Prince-Bishopric of Passau</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Erla, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.2,14.57&amp;year=1190" aria-label="Erla on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4889 477 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Wolfger von Erla"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5339 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5339 747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.2,14.57&amp;year=1190">What was here in 1190? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
